<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Rastatt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">7 March <a href="/years/1714">1714</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Peace of Rastatt · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rastatt">Rastatt</a> was signed on 7 March 1714 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and Emperor Charles VI, ending their war in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a>; the Empire as a whole made peace at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> later that year. Completing the Utrecht settlement, it gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> the Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, while France kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a> and Strasbourg.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-rastatt.jpg" alt="Treaty of Rastatt" width="320" height="218" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rebel Redcoat · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Europe,_1714.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
